WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.420
Most hormones knock at the cell surface and start a relay inside.

2
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.360
What happens when a hormone slips straight through the membrane?

3
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.160
Steroid hormones like cortisol and estradiol are hydrophobic, so they cross unaided.

4
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:21.773
With no surface relay, the cell catches the hormone inside.

5
00:00:23.173 --> 00:00:26.513
One protein binds the hormone and grips DNA directly.

6
00:00:27.913 --> 00:00:33.093
A protein controlling whether genes copy into RNA is a transcription factor.

7
00:00:34.373 --> 00:00:36.753
Here, the receptor itself is that factor.

8
00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:43.047
Humans have 48 nuclear receptor genes, and most share one modular plan.

9
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:48.927
A ligand is the small molecule a receptor binds, like a hormone or lipid.

10
00:00:50.287 --> 00:00:55.047
One end carries a switching surface called AF-1, and the other holds the ligand

11
00:00:55.047 --> 00:00:59.887
pocket. Between them, a domain folded around zinc reads DNA.

12
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.740
Most nuclear receptors work in pairs.

13
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:09.300
Their site on DNA, called a response element, has two matching halves.

14
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.260
Ligand binding and DNA binding sit in separate modules.

15
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.020
So one protein links a signal directly to chosen genes.

16
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:23.193
Before hormone arrives, how does a receptor wait?

17
00:01:24.373 --> 00:01:25.973
The family uses two routes.

18
00:01:27.513 --> 00:01:33.813
Glucocorticoid and androgen receptors wait mostly in the cytoplasm, held by Hsp90

19
00:01:33.813 --> 00:01:39.693
chaperones. A chaperone holds a protein's shape, keeping the pocket ready and the

20
00:01:39.693 --> 00:01:40.973
receptor off DNA.

21
00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:46.047
When cortisol enters the pocket, the chaperone group rearranges.

22
00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:50.707
The receptor enters the nucleus and sheds its chaperones.

23
00:01:52.007 --> 00:01:54.727
Then it pairs with an identical copy on DNA.

24
00:01:56.367 --> 00:02:01.027
Estrogen receptors use the same chaperones, though most already wait inside the

25
00:02:01.027 --> 00:02:02.027
nucleus.

26
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.560
Thyroid hormone receptors take a second route.

27
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.360
They stay in the nucleus on DNA, paired with a partner called RXR.

28
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.640
Picture an empty thyroid receptor on DNA without hormone.

29
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.540
Is that gene at normal baseline, or quieter than with no receptor at all?

30
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:33.790
Even quieter, because the empty receptor holds a co-repressor, a helper that keeps

31
00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:40.170
the gene off. Co-repressors bring enzymes that strip acetyl tags from histones, the

32
00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.290
proteins DNA wraps around.

33
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.190
The DNA packs tighter and blocks RNA polymerase, an active off switch.

34
00:02:49.283 --> 00:02:54.563
When thyroid hormone binds, the receptor drops its co-repressor and recruits a

35
00:02:54.563 --> 00:03:01.943
co-activator. Co-activators add acetyl tags back to histones, loosening the DNA so

36
00:03:01.943 --> 00:03:03.003
transcription starts.

37
00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:08.783
Other family members sense lipids like fatty acids or bile acids.

38
00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:15.363
In this route, a nuclear receptor binds both ligand and DNA to control
